Project planning divided the work into an eight-week schedule. The first week is used to finalize requirements, research existing tools, and set up the development environment. The second week covers wireframes and database design, and the third and fourth weeks are devoted to the backend, authentication, and the question-flow engine. In the fifth week the AI is integrated and the prompts are tuned, and the sixth week adds editing, explanations, and export features. The seventh week is reserved for testing and bug fixing, and the final week is for documentation and demo preparation. Responsibilities are divided among a project lead, frontend and backend developers, a prompt engineer, a tester, and a documentation owner, and in a solo or small-team project one person covers several of these roles in sequence. The main risks were identified early. Incorrect or unsafe AI output is the most serious and is reduced through template grounding, validation, and disclaimers. Data breaches are reduced through encryption and by storing as little personal data as possible. API cost or downtime is managed through caching and error handling, differences in law between regions are handled by starting with a single jurisdiction, and schedule slippage is handled by focusing first on a minimum viable product. The MVP includes three document types, an NDA, a rental agreement, and a freelance contract, in English and for one jurisdiction.
